---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { SECTIONS } from "../data/sections";

const title = "ProForm Treadmill Troubleshooting: Reset, Belt and iFIT Fixes";
const description =
  "ProForm treadmill troubleshooting: the safety key, console reset, a frozen iFIT screen, a belt that will not move or slips, incline faults and calling support.";

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 1, name: "Home", item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/" },
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 2, name: "Fault Diagnosis", item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/problems/" },
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 3, name: "ProForm Treadmill Troubleshooting", item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/proform-treadmill-troubleshooting/" },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How do I reset a ProForm treadmill?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Remove the safety key, switch the machine off, unplug it at the wall and wait several minutes, then plug in, switch on, let the console boot fully and insert the key. Also check the reset breaker next to the power inlet has not tripped. Console factory resets use a pinhole button or a button sequence that differs by model, so confirm it in your manual.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Why will my ProForm treadmill not turn on?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Work from the wall inwards: test the outlet with a lamp, plug straight into the wall rather than an extension, check the power cord is fully in, check the power switch and the reset breaker beside it, confirm the safety key is in and seated, and reseat the console cable if the machine was recently moved or assembled. Then try a proper power cycle.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Why does my ProForm treadmill turn on but the belt will not move?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Check the safety key first. Then, with the machine unplugged, confirm the belt moves by hand and nothing is jammed. If the motor spins but the belt does not, the drive belt has slipped or broken. If the motor does not try to run at all with the console lit, the motor controller is not sending power, which is a support call or a technician job.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How do I recalibrate the incline on a ProForm treadmill?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Most ProForm consoles have a calibration routine that runs the incline to its full extent and back to zero to relearn the end points. It is entered by a button sequence at power-up on LCD consoles or from a settings menu on touchscreens, with nobody on the deck and nothing beneath it. The sequence differs by model, so confirm it in your manual before trying one.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Why is my ProForm iFIT screen frozen or stuck on update?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Do a full power cycle with the key out and the machine unplugged for several minutes, then wait for the complete boot. An update on a weak Wi-Fi connection can take a long time, so give it longer than feels reasonable before intervening. If it freezes repeatedly at the same point, try the console reset for your model or contact iFIT support, who can push the update.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Is ProForm the same as NordicTrack?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "They are sister brands owned by the same parent company, iFIT Health and Fitness, and they share consoles, controllers, incline systems and the iFIT platform. ProForm is positioned as the more affordable range. Most troubleshooting and reset procedures carry across between the two, which is why NordicTrack guides are often useful to ProForm owners, though the exact button sequences still differ by model.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Why does my ProForm treadmill keep tripping the breaker?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Usually because it shares a circuit or sits on an AFCI-protected outlet. ProForm's manuals ask for a grounded 120-volt outlet on a circuit of 15 amps or more with nothing else heavy on it, and a UL 1449 surge suppressor; Member Care can supply an arc filter if an AFCI breaker trips repeatedly. If the treadmill's own power switch trips, wait five minutes before resetting it.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Where is the serial number on a ProForm treadmill?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "On a decal on the frame, not the console, usually low down near the front of the base or the power inlet. The front cover of the ProForm user's manual shows the model number and a drawing of where the serial decal sits on your model. Have both numbers ready before contacting support.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Why will my ProForm not connect to Bluetooth?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Many ProForm consoles only support Bluetooth to the iFIT app and compatible heart rate monitors, so headphones and speakers may never pair. Pair a phone from inside the iFIT app with Bluetooth on, keep it close to the console, and power cycle the treadmill if it disappears. Check your model's manual for what its console supports.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Is my ProForm treadmill still under warranty?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "It depends on the model and when you bought it. ProForm's website lists a lifetime motor warranty with 1, 3 or 5-year parts and labour options on current treadmills, while one current manual lists a 10-year frame and 1-year parts and labour. The cover usually applies to the original purchaser only, so check the warranty document that came with your machine.",
          },
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      description: description,
      author: { "@type": "Person", "@id": "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/editorial-standards/#shaheer-ali-khan", name: "Shaheer Ali Khan", honorificSuffix: "PhD", jobTitle: "Exercise Physiology & Biomechanics Specialist", url: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/editorial-standards/#who", sameAs: ["https://www.linkedin.com/in/shaheer-ali-khan-data/"], worksFor: { "@type": "Organization", name: "Treadmill Reviews USA" } },
      datePublished: "2026-10-08",
      dateModified: "2026-10-08",
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "Treadmill Reviews USA" },
    },
  ],
};

// "More in" links: the other pages in this article's hub group.
const HERE = "/proform-treadmill-troubleshooting/";
const HUB = SECTIONS.find((s) => s.slug === "problems");
const GROUP = HUB?.groups.find((g) => g.pages.some((p) => p.url === HERE)) ?? HUB?.groups[0];
const MORE = (GROUP?.pages ?? []).filter((p) => p.url !== HERE).slice(0, 6);
---

<Layout
  title={`${title} | Treadmill Reviews USA`}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: "2026-10-08",
    modifiedTime: "2026-10-08",
    author: "Shaheer Ali Khan",
    section: "Fitness Equipment",
    tags: [
      "proform treadmill troubleshooting",
      "proform treadmill reset",
      "proform treadmill not working",
      "proform treadmill wont start",
      "proform treadmill problems",
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
  stickyCta={{ text: "Reset Your ProForm", link: "#reset" }}
>
  <article class="ed-article" data-template="editorial">
    <div class="ed-col">
      <nav class="ed-crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
        <a href="/problems/">Fault Diagnosis</a>
        <span aria-hidden="true">›</span>
        <span>Troubleshooting</span>
      </nav>
      <h1 class="ed-title">ProForm Treadmill Troubleshooting: Reset, Belt, Incline and iFIT Problems</h1>
      <p class="ed-dek">
        Most ProForm faults are one of a short list: a safety key not seated, a console that needs a proper reset, an iFIT screen stuck on something, a belt that slips or will not move, or an incline that has lost its place. Here is the order to check them in, what to do yourself, and what to have ready before you call support.
      </p>

      <!-- Byline -->
      <div class="ed-byline">
        <img src="/authors/shaheer-ali-khan.png" alt="Shaheer Ali Khan, PhD" width="44" height="44" />
        <div class="ed-byline-text">
          By <a href="/editorial-standards/#who" rel="author">Shaheer Ali Khan, PhD</a><br />
          Updated November 21, 2026 · Exercise Physiology & Biomechanics Specialist · ACSM-EP, CSCS<br />
          <span class="ed-method">Based on manufacturer manuals and service guidance, and the faults owners report most.</span>
        </div>
        <a href="#reset" class="btn-ghost">How to Reset It</a>
      </div>
      <p class="ed-disclosure">
        Nothing on this page is a paid placement, and it carries no affiliate links. <a href="/editorial-standards/">How we review.</a>
      </p>

      <div class="ed-body prose prose-lg">
        <p class="ed-lead">
          ProForm treadmills are built by iFIT Health and Fitness, the same parent company as
          NordicTrack, and the two brands share consoles, controllers, incline systems and a great
          deal of their troubleshooting. Many of the problems owners search for are not faults at
          all but a console that has gone to sleep, an update that has stalled or a safety key that
          is a millimetre out of place. The real faults — a dry deck, a slipping belt, an incline
          that has lost calibration, a controller that has given up — follow a predictable
          sequence, and this page works through it in the order that finds the cause fastest.
        </p>

        <nav class="ed-toc not-prose" aria-label="Jump to">
          <h2>Jump to</h2>
          <ol>
            <li><a href="#short-answer">ProForm Treadmill Not Working? The Short Answer</a></li>
            <li><a href="#safety-key">Start With the Safety Key</a></li>
            <li><a href="#reset">How to Reset a ProForm Treadmill</a></li>
            <li><a href="#wont-turn-on">ProForm Treadmill Will Not Turn On</a></li>
            <li><a href="#trips-breaker">ProForm Trips the Breaker or Shuts Off Mid-Workout</a></li>
            <li><a href="#console-sleep">Console Sleep, Wake-on-Power and a Screen That Looks Dead</a></li>
            <li><a href="#ifit-console">iFIT Console Problems: Frozen, Stuck on Update, No Wi-Fi</a></li>
            <li><a href="#speed-changes">ProForm Speed Changing by Itself or Slowing Under You</a></li>
            <li><a href="#belt-wont-move">Console Lit but the Belt Will Not Move</a></li>
            <li><a href="#belt-slipping">Belt Slipping, Hesitating or Jerking</a></li>
            <li><a href="#off-centre-noise">Belt Off-Centre, Squeaking and Other ProForm Noises</a></li>
            <li><a href="#incline">Incline Stuck, Wrong or Needing Calibration</a></li>
            <li><a href="#error-messages">Error Messages on a ProForm Console</a></li>
            <li><a href="#model-serial-warranty">ProForm Model Number, Serial Number, Parts and Warranty</a></li>
            <li><a href="#support">When to Call ProForm Support and What to Have Ready</a></li>
            <li><a href="#faq">Frequently asked questions</a></li>
          </ol>
        </nav>

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>ProForm Treadmill Not Working? The Short Answer</h2>
<p>
            When a <strong>ProForm treadmill is not working</strong>, the checks go in this order:
            the <strong>safety key</strong> is in, seated and the right one; the <strong>power
            switch and reset breaker</strong> near the power cord are on and have not tripped; the
            console is actually awake rather than asleep; a <strong>proper power cycle</strong> —
            unplugged for a few minutes with the key out — has been tried; and, for an iFIT
            touchscreen console, the screen is not simply waiting on an update or a Wi-Fi
            connection.
          </p>
          <p>
            That sequence resolves the large majority of "ProForm won't start" and "ProForm
            console frozen" searches without a tool being picked up. What remains splits into a
            few recognisable faults. A console that lights but a belt that will not move is usually
            the key, the drive belt or the motor controller. A belt that hesitates underfoot is a
            dry deck or a loose belt. An incline that is stuck, will not go to zero or reads wrong
            is usually a calibration job, occasionally a failed incline motor. And a console that
            will not get past a logo, an update or a login screen is a software or network problem
            that a reset or a wired check will usually clear.
          </p>
          <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8"><table class="min-w-[520px] w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">ProForm symptom</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Most likely cause</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">First fix</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Completely dead, no lights</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Outlet, tripped power switch, safety key</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Test the outlet with a lamp; reset the switch by the cord; reseat the key</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Cuts out mid-workout or trips the house breaker</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Shared or AFCI circuit, surge suppressor, overload</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Wait five minutes, reset; give it its own circuit</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Console lit, belt will not move</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Safety key, drive belt, motor controller</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Reseat the key; then call support before opening the hood</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Speed changes by itself or slows underfoot</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">An iFIT workout, an over-tight belt, the speed sensor</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Check what is running; test in manual mode</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Belt slips, jerks or drifts to one side</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Tension or tracking, then lubrication</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Adjust the rear roller bolts a little at a time</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Incline stuck or reads wrong</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Lost calibration, loose connector</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Run the incline calibration for your model</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">iFIT screen frozen, no Wi-Fi, no Bluetooth</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Software, signal, unsupported device</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Full power cycle; check signal; pair through the iFIT app</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">New squeak, thump or grind</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Loose upright bolts, belt seam, rollers</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Tighten the upright bolts; time the noise to the belt</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p>
            Because ProForm and NordicTrack share so much, our guide to <a href="/how-to-reset-nordictrack-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">how to reset a NordicTrack treadmill</a> applies almost word for word to most ProForm consoles, and it is worth reading
            alongside this page, as is our full <a href="/nordictrack-treadmill-troubleshooting/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">NordicTrack treadmill troubleshooting</a> guide, which also covers decline and the iFIT features that change speed by themselves. Owners of other brands should use the matching guide, because belt, lube and power rules differ: <a href="/sole-treadmill-troubleshooting/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Sole</a>, <a href="/horizon-treadmill-troubleshooting/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Horizon</a> and <a href="/bowflex-treadmill-troubleshooting/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Bowflex treadmill troubleshooting</a>. If none of the steps here finds the cause, the last section sets
            out when to call ProForm support and what to have in front of you when you do; and if
            the question is whether the machine is worth keeping, our <a href="/proform-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">ProForm treadmill guide</a> covers the range and its common strengths and weaknesses.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="safety-key" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Start With the Safety Key</h2>
<p>
            Every ProForm treadmill has a magnetic safety key that sits in a recess on the console
            with a cord that clips to your clothing. With the key out, the machine will not run,
            and on many models the console will not light at all or will show a prompt to insert
            it. That is deliberate, and it is the first thing to check for any fault that looks
            like a dead machine.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Is it in?</strong> Keys get removed after sessions and put in drawers, and
            family members remove them to keep children off the machine. Ask before diagnosing.</li>
            <li><strong>Is it seated?</strong> Pull it out and push it back in firmly. A key sitting
            fractionally proud of the recess will not register.</li>
            <li><strong>Is the recess clean?</strong> Unplug the machine and look into the recess
            with a torch. Lint, hair or dust is enough to hold the key away from the switch. Clean
            it with a dry cloth or soft brush, not with anything metal or wet.</li>
            <li><strong>Is it the right key?</strong> Keys differ between brands and sometimes
            between models. A key that came with a second-hand machine or from another treadmill in
            the house may fit the recess without working.</li>
            <li><strong>Has the magnet weakened?</strong> A key that only works when pressed, or that
            cuts the machine out when the cord is tugged by an arm swing, has a magnet that is
            barely holding the switch. Replace it.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Our guide to a <a href="/treadmill-safety-key-not-working/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill safety key that is not working</a> covers each of these in detail and explains why bypassing the key with a loose magnet
            is a bad idea. If the key is in, seated and clean and the console still shows nothing,
            move on to the reset.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="reset" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>How to Reset a ProForm Treadmill</h2>
<p>
            "Reset" means three different things on a ProForm, and it is worth knowing which one
            you are doing.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">The reset breaker near the power cord</h3>
          <p>
            Most ProForm treadmills have a small button or switch next to the power inlet,
            sometimes labelled reset, that is a circuit breaker protecting the machine. If it has
            tripped — after a surge, a stall or an overload — the machine is dead until it is reset.
            Switch the <a href="/how-to-turn-on-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>power switch</a> off, press the reset button in or flip it back, wait a moment
            and switch on again. A breaker that trips again straight away is a fault, not something
            to keep resetting.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">The power cycle</h3>
          <p>
            This is the reset that fixes most console problems, and it has to be done properly.
            Remove the safety key. Switch the machine off at its power switch. Unplug it at the
            wall. Wait several minutes rather than several seconds; the console and controller hold
            charge, and a brief unplug does not clear them. Plug back in, switch on, wait for the
            console to boot fully, then insert the key. Owners who report that "unplugging did not
            work" have very often unplugged for ten seconds with the key still in.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">The console reset</h3>
          <p>
            Many ProForm consoles have a way to reset the console software itself — on some it is a
            small pinhole reset button on the back or underside of the console, on others a
            sequence of buttons held during power-up, and on iFIT touchscreens a factory reset
            option in the settings or a hold-button sequence at boot. We deliberately do not
            publish a single sequence here, because they differ between models and an incorrect
            one does nothing at best and enters a calibration or engineering mode at worst. Find
            your model number — on a sticker on the frame, usually near the front or the power
            inlet, not on the console — and confirm the procedure in the manual or on the ProForm
            support site. A factory reset on an iFIT console will usually remove the user's login
            and settings, so have the account details ready.
          </p>
          <p>
            Try the power cycle first. If the console comes back but the fault returns, try the
            console reset. If neither helps, the fault is not software.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="wont-turn-on" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>ProForm Treadmill Will Not Turn On</h2>
<p>
            A ProForm that is completely dark — no console lights, no response to the power switch
            — has lost power somewhere between the wall and the console, or it is being told not to
            run. Work from the wall inwards.
          </p>
          <ol>
            <li><strong>The outlet.</strong> Plug a lamp into the same socket. Check the breaker in
            the house panel and any GFCI outlet on the circuit. If the treadmill is on an <a href="/treadmill-extension-cord/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>extension
            cord</a> or power strip, plug it directly into the wall.</li>
            <li><strong>The power cord.</strong> Confirm it is fully pushed into the inlet on the
            treadmill; it works loose when the machine is moved or folded. Look for damage along its
            length.</li>
            <li><strong>The power switch and reset breaker</strong> next to the inlet, as above.</li>
            <li><strong>The safety key</strong>, because on many ProForm consoles a missing key means
            a dark console rather than a prompt.</li>
            <li><strong>The console cable.</strong> On a folding ProForm, the cable from the base to
            the console runs up inside one of the uprights and connects behind the console. It can
            work loose, particularly after assembly or a move. Unplug the machine, remove the console
            screws if the manual shows how, and reseat the connector. Do not force anything.</li>
            <li><strong>Sleep mode.</strong> Some consoles go dark after a period of inactivity and
            wake on a button press rather than on the power switch. See the next section.</li>
          </ol>
          <p>
            If power is reaching the machine — the power switch lights, or you can hear a relay
            click when it is switched on — but the console stays dark, the fault is in the console,
            its cable or the controller's supply to it. Our guide to a <a href="/treadmill-wont-turn-on/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill that won't turn on</a> goes through this in the general case, and our guide to a <a href="/treadmill-console-not-working/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill console that is not working</a> covers the upright cable and the console board specifically. A dark console with power
            confirmed at the machine is usually the point at which a call to support is the
            efficient next step.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="trips-breaker" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>ProForm Trips the Breaker or Shuts Off Mid-Workout</h2>
<p>
            A ProForm that runs for a while and then goes dead, or that takes the house breaker with
            it, is nearly always a power-supply problem rather than a broken treadmill. There are two
            breakers to think about: the small power switch or reset breaker on the treadmill
            frame beside the cord, and the breaker for the circuit in your electrical panel.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>When the treadmill&rsquo;s own switch trips.</strong> ProForm&rsquo;s user&rsquo;s
            manual for model PFTL90924.1 (checked November 2026) sets out the order for &ldquo;the power
            turns off during use&rdquo;: check the power switch and, if it has tripped, wait five
            minutes before pressing it back in; make sure the cord is plugged in, and if it is, unplug
            it, wait five minutes and plug it back in; then remove the key and reinsert it. If it still
            will not run, contact Member Care. Note the five-minute wait: pressing the switch straight
            back in is the habit that turns a one-off trip into a repeat.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>When the house breaker trips.</strong> A treadmill motor draws a surge as it
            starts and climbs under load, and it does not share a circuit well. The same manual asks
            for a grounded outlet on a nominal 120-volt circuit capable of carrying 15 or more amps,
            with nothing else on that circuit except low-power devices such as phone chargers, and
            for the treadmill to be plugged into a UL 1449 listed surge suppressor that meets the
            specification in the manual. It also notes that if the treadmill is on an AFCI-protected
            outlet and the breaker trips repeatedly, Member Care can supply an arc filter. Newer homes
            often have AFCI breakers on bedroom and living-room circuits, which is a common reason a
            new ProForm trips a breaker that an old one never did.
          </p>
          <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8"><table class="min-w-[520px] w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">What trips</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">When</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Usual reason</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">What to do</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Treadmill power switch</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">After a long or heavy session</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Overload: dry or over-tight belt, high incline</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Wait five minutes, reset; check tension and lubrication guidance</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">House breaker</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">The moment the belt starts</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Shared circuit, or an AFCI or GFCI device</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Move to its own circuit; ask Member Care about an arc filter</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">House breaker</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Only when another appliance runs</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Circuit overloaded</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Separate the loads; no space heaters on the same circuit</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Either, with a burning smell</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Any time</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Motor, controller or wiring fault</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Stop, unplug and call support</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p>
            Avoid a long extension lead or a household power strip in place of the specified surge
            suppressor; both drop voltage and make trips more likely. Our guide to a <a href="/treadmill-trips-breaker/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill that trips the breaker</a> goes through the circuit checks in the general case, and our <a href="/treadmill-surge-protector/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill surge protector guide</a> explains what the joule and clamping ratings mean. Anything beyond resetting and moving
            the plug, such as changing a breaker or adding a circuit, is a job for an electrician.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="console-sleep" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Console Sleep, Wake-on-Power and a Screen That Looks Dead</h2>
<p>
            A good proportion of "my ProForm console is dead" reports are consoles doing exactly
            what they were set to do. It is worth understanding the behaviour before assuming a
            fault.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Sleep mode.</strong> Both the simpler LCD consoles and the iFIT touchscreens
            go to sleep after a period without input. On an LCD console the display blanks; on a
            touchscreen the screen goes dark. Pressing a button, tapping the screen or, on some
            models, inserting the safety key wakes it. A console that is asleep looks identical to a
            console with no power, and the difference is whether it responds to a press.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Boot time.</strong> An iFIT touchscreen is a small computer and takes a while
            to boot after power is applied — sometimes long enough that an impatient owner
            switches off and on again before it has finished, which starts the boot over. After
            switching on, wait, and watch for the logo.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Wake on power.</strong> Some touchscreen consoles have a setting that decides
            whether the screen turns on automatically when the machine is powered at the switch or
            waits for a tap. If a console that used to light up now stays dark until touched, or
            the other way round, look for that setting rather than assuming a fault.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The screen is on but black.</strong> A touchscreen that is lit — you can see a
            faint glow in a dark room — but shows nothing is usually a software hang, and the power
            cycle clears it. A screen that is completely unlit on a machine with confirmed power is
            a different matter and points at the console cable or the console itself.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Standby draw.</strong> A touchscreen console that is left plugged in keeps the
            computer in standby, which is why some owners find the screen warm when the machine has
            not been used. Switching off at the power switch between sessions is reasonable; it will
            just mean a full boot next time.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="ifit-console" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>iFIT Console Problems: Frozen, Stuck on Update, No Wi-Fi</h2>
<p>
            ProForm's touchscreen consoles run iFIT, and a class of problems exists on them that
            the simpler LCD consoles do not have. They are software and network problems, and they
            are approached differently from mechanical faults.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">A frozen screen</h3>
          <p>
            A screen that does not respond to touch, or is stuck on the logo or a spinning
            indicator, needs a proper power cycle: key out, power off, unplugged for several
            minutes, power on, wait for the full boot. If it freezes again at the same point, note
            exactly what is on the screen and try the console reset for your model. A screen that
            freezes only during workouts, or only when streaming video, is more likely a network
            or memory issue than a hardware fault.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Stuck on an update</h3>
          <p>
            iFIT consoles download updates, and an update that stalls — on a slow connection, or
            when the machine is switched off part-way through — can leave the console looping or
            sitting on a progress screen for a very long time. Give it longer than feels
            reasonable, with a good Wi-Fi signal, before intervening. If it has made no progress
            after a long wait, power cycle and let it try again. If it fails repeatedly, support can
            usually push the update or walk you through recovering the console.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Wi-Fi and login</h3>
          <p>
            A console that cannot connect usually has a weak signal where the treadmill sits, a
            router that needs restarting, or a network setting it does not like. Check the signal
            with a phone standing on the deck; move the router or add an extender if it is poor.
            Forget the network on the console and reconnect. Restart the router. If the console
            connects but will not log in, the iFIT account itself — password, subscription status —
            is the place to look, and that is an account question rather than a treadmill fault.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Bluetooth will not connect</h3>
          <p>
            Bluetooth on a ProForm console is narrower than owners expect. The user&rsquo;s manual
            for ProForm model PFTL90924.1 (checked November 2026) says its console supports
            Bluetooth connections to smart devices through the iFIT app and to compatible heart rate
            monitors, and that other Bluetooth connections are not supported. On a console like
            that, headphones or a speaker that will not pair are not a fault. Other consoles,
            particularly touchscreens, may differ, so check the manual for your model number.
          </p>
          <p>
            For a phone or tablet, pair from inside the iFIT app with Bluetooth switched on in the
            phone&rsquo;s settings, rather than from the phone&rsquo;s own Bluetooth menu. Close the
            app fully and reopen it, keep the phone close to the console, and power cycle the
            treadmill if the console no longer appears. The same manual notes that the console
            requires activation before its connected features work, which catches out owners of
            second-hand machines. A chest strap that will not show a heart rate is usually a strap
            battery, a dry strap or a second device holding the connection; our guide to a <a href="/treadmill-heart-rate-not-working/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill heart rate monitor that is not working</a> covers those checks.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Using the machine without iFIT</h3>
          <p>
            A ProForm touchscreen console can be used in a manual mode without a subscription, with
            speed and incline controlled directly, though the exact way to reach it varies by console
            and some owners find it awkward. If you are weighing whether to keep paying, our guide to <a href="/is-ifit-worth-it/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">whether iFIT is worth it</a> sets out what you get and what you lose; check the current price rather than relying on
            any figure you have seen quoted.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="speed-changes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>ProForm Speed Changing by Itself or Slowing Under You</h2>
<p>
            Three different complaints get searched as &ldquo;ProForm speed problem&rdquo;, and
            they have different causes: the speed changes without you touching anything, the belt
            slows or stutters each time a foot lands, or the machine will not go as fast as you ask.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Speed or incline changing on its own.</strong> On an iFIT console, check what is
            on the screen before anything else. Trainer-led classes and map routes are built to
            control the treadmill, so speed and incline will move during them. Press the speed or
            incline buttons to override, or start a manual workout when you want the machine to stay
            where you put it. If you want to understand which iFIT features adjust the machine and
            how to turn them off, the <a href="/nordictrack-treadmill-troubleshooting/#changes-by-itself" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">NordicTrack section on speed and incline changing by itself</a> applies to ProForm&rsquo;s iFIT consoles too. If the speed surges or drops in manual mode,
            with nothing running, that is a fault, and a belt that will not slow down or stop when
            the key is pulled is a reason to stop using the machine and call support.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The belt slows when you walk on it.</strong> ProForm&rsquo;s manual for model
            PFTL90924.1 (checked November 2026) lists two checks for this symptom: that the
            treadmill is on a surge suppressor meeting its specification, and that the belt is not
            over-tightened. An over-tight belt loads the motor at every footstrike. The manual&rsquo;s
            guide is that, with the key out and the cord unplugged, you should be able to lift each
            edge of the walking belt 2 to 3 inches off the platform; if it is tighter than that,
            loosen both rear roller screws by a quarter-turn and test. If the belt still slows, it
            directs owners to Member Care.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Speed reading wrong, or capped.</strong> A console that shows a speed the belt
            plainly is not doing, or that drops to zero and stops the belt a few seconds into a
            workout, is often the speed sensor at the front roller: a magnet that has moved, a dirty
            sensor face or a loose wire. Our <a href="/treadmill-speed-sensor/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill speed sensor guide</a> covers the checks, all with the machine unplugged. If the top speed seems low, compare it
            with the maximum listed for your model before suspecting a fault, and check the console is
            not set to a different unit of measurement; our guide on <a href="/is-my-treadmill-speed-accurate/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">whether your treadmill speed is accurate</a> explains how to time the belt yourself.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="belt-wont-move" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Console Lit but the Belt Will Not Move</h2>
<p>
            The console works, you press start, and either nothing happens or the motor hums
            without the belt moving. This is one of the more common ProForm complaints and it has a
            short list of causes.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>The safety key</strong>, again. On consoles that stay lit without the key,
            a missing or unseated key produces a console that works and a belt that does not,
            usually with a prompt on the display. Check it first.</li>
            <li><strong>Something physically stopping the belt.</strong> An object under the belt,
            a belt jammed against a side rail, or a belt so tight after a tensioning attempt that the
            motor cannot turn it. With the machine unplugged, try moving the belt by hand.</li>
            <li><strong>The drive belt.</strong> If the motor runs — you hear it spin up — but the
            walking belt does not move, the short ribbed belt between motor and front roller has
            slipped off, snapped or is slipping on its pulleys. Unplug, remove the motor hood and
            look. This is a replaceable part, but ProForm&rsquo;s manuals say not to remove the
            motor hood unless an authorised service representative tells you to, so on a machine
            in warranty, call before you open it.</li>
            <li><strong>The motor controller.</strong> If the motor does not try to run at all with
            the key in and the console lit, the controller is not sending it power. A tripped reset
            breaker, a blown fuse on the board, a failed board after a surge or a loose motor
            connector are the usual reasons. Scorch marks or a burnt smell under the hood mean stop
            and call a technician.</li>
            <li><strong>The motor itself.</strong> Less common, but a motor with worn brushes can
            fail to start under load while still humming. A motor that is seized will not turn by
            hand with the drive belt removed.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Our guide to a <a href="/treadmill-turns-on-but-belt-wont-move/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill that turns on but the belt will not move</a> works through these with the checks for each. On a ProForm that is still within its
            parts warranty, a controller or motor fault is a support call rather than a purchase;
            check your warranty document before buying anything, because opening the motor hood
            for an inspection is one thing and replacing a board is another.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="belt-slipping" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Belt Slipping, Hesitating or Jerking</h2>
<p>
            A belt that pauses for an instant when your foot lands, jerks at low speed or drifts
            to one side is a maintenance problem rather than a fault, and on a ProForm it is almost
            always one of three things.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>A dry deck.</strong> A deck that has run dry makes the belt drag so that the
            motor momentarily loses the fight with your foot. But check your manual before adding
            anything: current ProForm manuals we checked in November 2026 (for example the user's
            manual for model PFTL90924.1) say the walking belt comes coated with a high-performance
            lubricant and that you should never apply silicone spray or anything else to the belt or
            platform unless an authorised service representative tells you to, and to contact
            Member Care if you think it needs more. Older models and some other ProForm treadmills
            do specify periodic lubrication. Follow the instruction for your model number; where the
            manual says lubricate, use the lubricant and the quantity it specifies.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Belt tension.</strong> If the deck is lubricated and the belt still slips under
            a firm foot plant, it may be loose. The two bolts at the rear of the deck tension the
            belt; turn each the same amount, a quarter-turn at a time, and test between turns. A
            belt that is too tight makes the motor run hot and wears the rollers, so stop at the
            point where the slip goes, not beyond.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Tracking.</strong> A belt that drifts left or right is corrected with the same
            rear bolts, turned individually. Run the belt at a slow speed with nobody on it, turn
            the bolt on the side the belt has drifted towards a quarter-turn, wait, and repeat until
            it runs centrally.
          </p>
          <p>
            A belt that slips with the deck lubricated and the tension correct is a stretched belt,
            a <a href="/treadmill-deck-replacement/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>worn deck</a> surface or a glazed drive belt. For the two fixes in order, see <a href="/treadmill-belt-lubrication/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">how to lube a treadmill</a> and <a href="/how-to-adjust-treadmill-belt/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">how to tighten a treadmill belt</a>. Our guide to a <a href="/treadmill-belt-slipping/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill belt that slips</a> goes through the sequence, including the lift test for tension and when a belt is past
            adjusting. Do not simply keep tightening: a drum-tight belt on a dry deck is one of the
            more reliable ways to shorten the life of a ProForm motor.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="off-centre-noise" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Belt Off-Centre, Squeaking and Other ProForm Noises</h2>
<p>
            A belt that has crept to one side and a new noise often arrive together, because the
            same things cause both: a machine that has been moved, folded or assembled with bolts left
            loose, and a belt running out of true.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Centring the belt</h3>
          <p>
            The ProForm manual for model PFTL90924.1 (checked November 2026) gives a centring
            procedure that is worth knowing because it differs from the generic advice. With the key
            removed and the power cord unplugged, it uses only the <em>left</em> rear idler roller
            screw: if the belt has shifted to the left, turn that screw clockwise half a turn; if it has
            shifted to the right, turn it counter-clockwise half a turn. Then plug back in, insert the
            key, walk on the treadmill for a few minutes and repeat until the belt runs centrally,
            taking care not to over-tighten it. Other ProForm models may use both screws, so follow the
            drawing in your own manual. Our step-by-step guide on <a href="/how-to-center-treadmill-belt/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">how to center a treadmill belt</a> explains why the belt moves the way it does and how to avoid chasing it from side to side.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Placing a noise by its timing</h3>
          <p>
            For a squeak during use, the same manual&rsquo;s first step is to retighten the screws
            that secure the uprights, which loosen with use and after a move. For anything else, the
            timing of the noise is the best clue to its source.
          </p>
          <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8"><table class="min-w-[520px] w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">The noise</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Most likely source</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">What to do</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Squeak in time with your steps</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Upright or base bolts working loose</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Retighten the upright screws, then the base</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Thump once per belt revolution</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Belt seam, damaged belt, flat spot on a roller</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Mark the belt and watch it come round</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Rubbing or scuffing at one side</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Belt off-centre, touching a rail</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Centre it as your manual shows</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Whine rising with speed</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Motor or drive belt</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Note the speed it starts at; call support</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Grinding or rumbling</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Roller bearings or motor bearings</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Stop using it and have it checked</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Clunk when the incline moves</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Incline motor, its mounts, or a loose frame bolt</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Recalibrate; check the incline mounts with power off</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p>
            Our guides to a <a href="/treadmill-squeaking-noise/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill squeaking noise</a> and a <a href="/treadmill-thumping-noise/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill thumping noise</a> go through each in more detail, and a machine that rocks or wobbles is covered under <a href="/treadmill-shaking-or-wobbling/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill shaking or wobbling</a>.
            Always unplug the treadmill before checking bolts, rollers or anything under the deck, and
            do not put hands near the rear roller while the belt is moving.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="incline" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Incline Stuck, Wrong or Needing Calibration</h2>
<p>
            ProForm inclines are driven by a motor under the deck or at the front of the frame,
            with a sensor that tells the console where the deck is. Three faults are common.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The incline will not move at all.</strong> Check that the machine is not in a
            mode that locks incline, that the safety key is in, and that nothing is under the deck
            or in the path of the incline mechanism. Then, with the machine unplugged, look at the
            incline motor connector at the base. A loose connector after a move is a common finding.
            An incline motor that hums without moving, or that is silent when the console calls for
            incline, is a motor or controller fault.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The incline moves but reads wrong or will not return to zero.</strong> The
            console has lost track of where the deck is. This typically happens after a power
            interruption during an incline change, after a console or controller replacement, or
            after the machine has been moved while powered. The fix is to recalibrate.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The incline hunts or moves on its own.</strong> The position sensor is giving an
            unstable reading, or the console is trying and failing to reach a calibrated position.
            Recalibrate first; if it persists, the sensor or its wiring is suspect.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Incline calibration</h3>
          <p>
            Most ProForm consoles have a calibration routine in which the machine runs the incline
            to its full extent and back to zero, relearning the end points from the position
            sensor. It is entered with a button sequence during power-up on LCD consoles or from a
            settings or maintenance menu on touchscreens, and it takes a minute or two with nobody
            on the deck. The exact way in differs by model, and some sequences also calibrate speed
            or reset other settings, so confirm the procedure for your model number in the manual
            or on the support site rather than trying a sequence found for a different machine.
            Make sure nothing is under the deck before running it; the deck will go to its lowest
            position. Our guide to a <a href="/treadmill-incline-not-working/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill incline that is not working</a> covers the incline motor, the sensor and the wiring in the general case.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="error-messages" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Error Messages on a ProForm Console</h2>
<p>
            ProForm consoles report faults with codes, letters or short messages, and the
            temptation is to search for the code and act on the first table that comes up. We do
            not list codes and meanings here, deliberately. ProForm has used many console designs
            over the years, the same code does not mean the same thing across them, and a table
            compiled for one model will send the owner of another to replace a part that was never
            at fault.
          </p>
          <p>
            What is reliable is the context. Write down the exact message, whether it appeared at
            power-up, immediately on pressing start, a few seconds after the belt began moving, or
            minutes into a session, and what the belt and incline were doing at the time. Then look
            the message up for your model number — on the frame sticker — in the manual or on the
            ProForm support site, which lists messages by model.
          </p>
          <p>
            Some general patterns do hold. A message at power-up, before anything moves, is usually
            the console failing to talk to the controller in the base: a loose console cable or a
            controller fault. A message immediately on start is often the safety key or the
            controller's self-check. A message a few seconds after the belt starts is characteristic
            of the controller not seeing a speed signal from the sensor on the front roller. A
            message after minutes of normal running points at overheating, an overcurrent from a
            dry deck, or an intermittent connection. A message during an incline change is the
            incline system.
          </p>
          <p>
            Clear the message with a proper power cycle before anything else. Many messages are a
            single event — a momentary voltage dip, a key that lost contact — and do not come back.
            A message that returns every time is a fault, and the pattern above tells you which part
            of the machine to look at. Our <a href="/treadmill-troubleshooting/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill troubleshooting guide</a> works through the general sequence by symptom.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="model-serial-warranty" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>ProForm Model Number, Serial Number, Parts and Warranty</h2>
<p>
            Almost every useful next step, from looking up an error to ordering a part to making a
            warranty claim, starts with the model and serial number. The name on the console is not
            enough: ProForm reuses names across model years, and the internal parts differ.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Finding the numbers</h3>
          <p>
            ProForm user&rsquo;s manuals print the model number on the front cover, with a drawing
            showing where the serial number decal sits on the machine and a space to write the serial
            number down (checked November 2026). The decal is on the frame rather than the console,
            and is usually low down near the front of the base or the power inlet; on a folding model
            you may need the deck down to see it. ProForm treadmill model numbers in the manuals we
            checked begin with PFTL, followed by digits and a revision number. If
            the manual is lost, the decal is the source of truth, and support can find the manual from
            the model number.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Getting parts and service</h3>
          <p>
            Current ProForm manuals direct owners to Member Care at my.iFIT.com for service, and to
            my.proform.com to register the product and activate the warranty. Parts are model-specific,
            so order by model number, and use manufacturer-supplied parts, which the manuals also ask
            for. Our guide to <a href="/treadmill-parts/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill parts</a> explains which parts wear and roughly what to expect.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">What the warranty covers</h3>
          <p>
            ProForm warranty terms vary by model and by when and where you bought the machine, so
            the document that came with yours is the one that counts. Two examples, both checked in
            November 2026, show how much they differ:
          </p>
          <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8"><table class="min-w-[520px] w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Source</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Treadmill warranty stated</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">ProForm&rsquo;s website, treadmills (current range)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Lifetime motor warranty plus 1, 3 or 5-year parts and labour options</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">User&rsquo;s manual, model PFTL90924.1</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Frame 10 years; parts and labour 1 year from purchase</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p>
            The same manual&rsquo;s limited warranty also says the cover is for the original
            purchaser only and is not transferable, that repairs must be pre-authorised by iFIT, that
            a small handling charge may apply to shipped parts and a trip charge to in-home service,
            and that the warranty can be voided by commercial or rental use, abuse, modification, use
            outside the USA, or not following the manual&rsquo;s instructions and warnings. That last
            point matters for troubleshooting: the manual also says not to remove the motor hood
            unless an authorised service representative tells you to. Inside the warranty period,
            call before you open it. Buying second-hand means you almost certainly have no warranty.
            Our <a href="/treadmill-warranty-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill warranty guide</a> explains how frame, motor, parts and labour cover compare across brands.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="support" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>When to Call ProForm Support and What to Have Ready</h2>
<p>
            There is a point at which carrying on alone costs more than it saves. Call ProForm or
            iFIT support when:
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li>The machine is within its warranty period for the part concerned. Check your
            warranty document; the cover for frame, motor, parts and labour differs, and opening
            the hood to inspect is not the same as replacing a board.</li>
            <li>Power is confirmed at the machine but the console stays dark.</li>
            <li>The console is lit and the key is in but the motor does not attempt to run, and the
            drive belt is intact.</li>
            <li>There is any <a href="/treadmill-burning-smell/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>burning smell</a>, scorching, or a breaker that trips on start-up.</li>
            <li>An iFIT console is stuck on an update or will not boot after a reset.</li>
            <li>An error message returns every time after a proper power cycle.</li>
            <li>The incline will not calibrate, or the incline motor does not respond.</li>
            <li>The belt surges or changes speed on its own, or does not stop when the key is
            pulled. Stop using the machine until it is fixed.</li>
          </ul>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Have this ready</h3>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>The model number</strong> from the sticker on the frame — near the front,
            the power inlet or under the motor hood — not the marketing name on the console. Several
            consoles share names across model years.</li>
            <li><strong>The serial number</strong>, from the same sticker.</li>
            <li><strong>Proof of purchase</strong> with the date, for any warranty question.</li>
            <li><strong>The exact error message</strong> and when it appears, written down.</li>
            <li><strong>A short video</strong> of the symptom on your phone: the belt hesitating,
            the console freezing, the noise. A video of the fault happening is worth more than
            a description of it, and support can often diagnose from it directly.</li>
            <li><strong>What you have already tried</strong>, so the call does not start from the
            beginning.</li>
            <li><strong>Your iFIT account email</strong> if the console is a touchscreen.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Out of warranty, support can still sell the correct part by model number, which is
            worth it for anything model-specific such as a controller board, and can tell you
            whether an authorised technician covers your area. For the repair-or-replace question
            on an older machine, the cost of the part plus a technician visit against the value of
            the treadmill is the deciding arithmetic, and our <a href="/treadmill-repair-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill repair guide</a> works through it.
          </p>
        </div>


        <!-- Common mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Mistakes to Avoid With ProForm Treadmill Troubleshooting</h2>
          <p>Most ProForm faults are simple. These four are how owners make them take longer or cost more than they should.</p>
          <ol class="ed-mistakes not-prose">
            <li>
              <h3>Unplugging for ten seconds and calling it a reset</h3>
              <p>The console and controller hold charge, and a brief unplug does not clear them. A proper power cycle is key out, power switch off, unplugged at the wall for several minutes, then power on and a full boot before the key goes back in. Most frozen screens and one-off error messages clear with this and not with a quick flick of the switch.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Trying a reset or calibration sequence from another model</h3>
              <p>Button sequences found online for a different ProForm or NordicTrack console may do nothing, or may enter an engineering mode and change settings you did not intend. Find the model number on the frame sticker, not the console name, and confirm the sequence in the manual or on the support site for that exact model before pressing anything.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Tightening the belt to cure a slip</h3>
              <p>Cranking the belt until the slip stops presses it onto a deck that may already be dragging, so the motor runs hotter and the rollers wear. Check the manual first: some ProForm belts are pre-lubricated and must not be sprayed, others need periodic lubricant. Then adjust tension a quarter-turn at a time on both rear bolts, stopping as soon as the slip is gone.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Calling support without the model number or a video</h3>
              <p>Several ProForm consoles share marketing names across years, so the name on the console is not enough. Have the model and serial number from the frame sticker, proof of purchase, the exact error message and a short phone video of the fault happening. A video lets support diagnose directly and saves a second call.</p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </div>

        <!-- The bottom line -->
        <div id="bottom-line" class="ed-bottomline">
          <h2>The fix, in short</h2>
          <p>
            <strong class="text-white">Key, breaker, proper power cycle, then the manual.</strong> Most ProForm problems are a safety key not seated, a tripped reset breaker, a console
            that needed a real unplug or an iFIT screen waiting on an update, and none of them need
            a tool.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong class="text-white">For anything else, diagnose before you pay.</strong> A slipping
            belt is tension or a dry deck, a wrong incline is a calibration, and a lit console with a dead belt
            is the drive belt or the controller. Confirm the procedure for your model number, have
            the sticker and a video ready for support, and read our <a href="/how-to-reset-nordictrack-treadmill/" class="text-[#5AA9FF] font-bold no-underline">NordicTrack reset guide</a> for the shared console steps.
          </p>
        </div>

        <aside class="ed-more not-prose">
          <div class="ed-more-k">Keep reading</div>
